  "id": "Compound::DB00665 - Disease::DOID:10283",
  "verbalization": "Nilutamide is hypothesized to have a potential role in the treatment of prostate cancer based on its structural and functional similarities to other androgen receptor inhibitors. The evidence suggests that Nilutamide, through its resemblance to Enzalutamide and Bicalutamide, which in turn treat prostate cancer, may share similar mechanisms of action. This is supported by the lowest common ancestors linking Nilutamide to these compounds, which are all part of the broader class of androgen receptor inhibitors. Additionally, Nilutamide's inclusion in the category of Androgen Receptor Antagonists further underscores its potential therapeutic relevance. The structural similarities between Nilutamide and other compounds like Nitisinone, which resemble Flutamide and are themselves linked to prostate cancer treatment, also contribute to the overall evidence supporting a relationship between Nilutamide and prostate cancer. The cumulative evidence suggests that Nilutamide may act through a mechanism involving androgen receptor inhibition, potentially offering a repurposed therapeutic approach for prostate cancer.",
